`logBytes` is exact where the log remembers the moment and blank where it does not. A new release gets
the honest thing, the size of `loop.log` when it is stamped. For the ones already out there the only
mark is the release's own `Released v<version>. It is now in the menu.` line, and the harness that
writes `loop.log` elides long tool output - so 21 of the 77 have that line and 56 do not. Those 56 get
no `logBytes` at all rather than a number invented out of a session quoting an older release or
interpolated from timestamps; the menu shows nothing for them, which is true. A quoted line is only
accepted where the log's own order allows it, `node tools/release-stats.js --check` says which entries
are still blank, and nothing the tool does ever rewrites a number that is already there - so it is
safe to run it any time, including after anyone has edited the file by hand. Counting is metadata for
the menu, so a counting failure warns and steps over rather than losing you a release.
