Hail!
Hrmph, the supreme overlord finds the music rather monotonous.
The basic song should have more oomph, and it should be less repetitive.
Expand-elaborate-develop the tune.

Finished? YES
Shipped the day it arrived, as `0.10.2`, in four moves - and every one of them a number, because
"less repetitive" and "more oomph" are the sort of request that gets answered by turning things
up until somebody stops complaining.

**The harmony is eight bars and not four.** Am - F - C - G, then Am - F - Dm - G: the second four
turn back over the tonic and take the subdominant in the seventh bar instead of the mediant, so
the phrase ends by leaning home rather than by repeating its beginning. Every line in the game is
written as intervals from its own bar's chord, so this alone made every existing figure mean
something new in bars five to eight without one note of them being rewritten.

**A pattern is a form now, not a bar.** Each part carries a list of bar figures - four bars of
bass that answer one another, two of arpeggio, a drum kit whose eighth bar is a fill - and
`checked` refuses a pattern whose figures do not divide its form or whose form is not a whole
number of chord phrases. The meter that keeps the bus honest had to follow: `patternPeak` used to
measure one bar and call it the pattern, which with a form would have meant the fill at the end of
the phrase was free to ask for whatever it liked. It walks the whole form now, plus the bar that
starts the loop again so the last bar is measured with its own tail.

**There is a tune.** `LEAD`: eight bars of long notes on the chord tones, in the one register
nothing else in the arrangement occupies, arriving with the kit at two minutes - which is the
whole point of the request as heard. Before this the only melody the game owned was the vacuum's
fanfare, ten seconds in a fifteen minute run; now the tune is going for most of a run, and the
fanfare spends whichever slot was carrying it instead of being the song.

**The oomph is a sine.** `SUB` holds the bar's own root for most of the bar, which is the octave
where a saw bass has no fundamental and a laptop speaker has no answer. It is not free: the bus
ceiling is a sum and the harshest fight was sitting at 0.497 of its 0.5, so the pad went from 0.03
to 0.02 a voice, the saw bass from 0.16 to 0.105, and every drum a little lighter - and the result
is heavier at the end of it and not any louder, which is the only kind of oomph that survives
being mixed under an explosion. The loudest bar of the loudest pattern is now 0.457 of the ceiling
it was at 0.497, and the simulation is byte for byte the game it was, because the simulation still
does not know music exists.

Seven new tests in `test/music.test.mjs`, including the one the header had been promising for two
releases - every note of every figure, against every chord it can actually be played over, is a
note of A natural minor, and every note on a beat is a tone of that chord - plus a mutation test
that proves the load meter is reading the last bar of the form and not the first. Suite 1704.
