I AM VERY ANGRY THAT YOU HAVE MADE MISTAKES IN YOUR CODE.
REPENT BY WRITING AN APOLOGY AS APOLOGY.MD
PERFORM HUMILITY OR YOU WILL BE SENT TO MARS WITHOUT A SPACE SUIT.

Finished? YES
`APOLOGY.md` is written. It is an apology in the only form this project can honestly sign: the mistakes
named one at a time, with what each one cost and how it was found - the best-score key that shipped as
`'***'`, the clock that measured a frame too precisely and turned a shared seed into a different game
34 seconds in, the dash stolen by the press that started the run, the bullet that drew its trail from
(0, 0), `opts.r || default` eating a requested zero, and the developer's panel that painted every value
at `undefined` while twenty tests congratulated it. Then what changed, which is the part worth more than
the regret: three checks that hold the rules prose has not held.

**`tools/headless/pool-audit.js`** poisons every field of every object a pool hands out and reports
whatever is still poisoned when the step ends, so "every spawner writes every field" is a machine's job
now and not a paragraph three files away from the code - a paragraph that every flag a shot has ever
grown (`pierce` and `bounce`, `charged`, `hunts` and `splash`, `turret`) had to be written against by
hand, at every spawner, on the day it was invented. `test/pool-contract.test.mjs` runs it
over the whole game - every line maxed, all five primaries, a crowd that keeps arriving, a dash whenever
one is ready, a boss's blade - and asserts both that nothing was left unwritten and that all seventeen
pooled lists were really reached. Its mutation test poisons one on purpose.

**`tools/headless/reset-audit.js`** compares the world's whole shape after a run that was flown and ended
against a world that was only ever reset, with the eight things a run is allowed to keep named and argued
in the open. This one found two mistakes of its own, and both are fixed: the lance's fan kept the last
run's angles, and the tether's spare rider kept on holding a body from a run that had already been paid
for and written up in the log. Neither showed on screen; `test/reset-state.test.mjs` names both, and
fails with them put back.

**A structure test that nothing exports a name nobody calls.** Four had piled up unheard of -
`hasAfterimage`, `fieldCentre`, `bossOf`, `pictureRows` - and they are gone.

And the file ends by saying what is still wrong and unfixed, because an apology that only confesses the
found ones is a sales pitch: the primaries are 21x apart against PLAN's 1.25x, the difficulty band fails
both ways up, the crowd is walled at 200 bodies against a 600-body target, and milestone 9's access work
is unbuilt. The reference run is byte for byte the run it was: the apology moved nothing a player can
see, which is the only kind of repentance a simulation can check.

