OUTSTANDING BUG, HOTFIX: The menu music does not start unless I hit H to enter the hangar. The menu
music should announce itself on the root menu. Thanketh thee for thy service.

Finished? YES
It announces itself now, in 0.10.15, and the reason it did not was ours rather than the browser's. The
audio device was only ever built by the first press that bothered to ask for one, and the press that
asks is almost always the one that launches a run - so a browser that would have let the title screen
make a sound on its own (one with no autoplay rule, or one that remembers you asking for sound here
before) was never once asked. The device is asked for at boot now, before the first frame goes by, and
where the browser says yes your song is playing before a hand has done anything at all.

Two things came with it. The bytes are now fetched only when the device can actually be heard, because
8.7 MB of samples queued onto a clock that is not running is a bill and not a song - and the sequencer
does the same, waiting for the wake instead of writing half a second of the title pad into a stopped
clock to be thrown at the present later. And `STARFALL.stats().menuTrack.reason` says out loud why the
title is quiet, in the two cases it can be: "asleep: no browser lets a page make a sound before a
press", or "this visit came back muted".

The one thing no page can do is be heard before a press by a browser whose rule is strict and whose
visitor is new. So the game now takes the first press of *any* kind as that press - including one the
game does not answer, so a stray key wakes the menu just as `H` did - and a visit that came back muted
gets its song the moment `M` lets the mute up, without having to go and open a panel first.

Three new tests and eight amended ones, and a stub browser that now models the autoplay rule at all:
`autoplay: true` is a browser that lets the page play, and without it the device is built asleep and
`resume()` is refused until the harness presses something. The reference run traces the same bytes as
before, which is the check that a device built a frame earlier moved nothing a player can feel.
